## Why This Question Is Asked
This question assesses understanding of multi-tenant fairness, resource allocation under constraints, and SLA guarantees during outage scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- **CKNE-to-operational knob mapping**: Translating urgency scores into actionable parameters—sampling frequency, queue capacity, and cooldown duration.
- **Priority scheduling with budget enforcement**: Allocating finite scheduler time while guaranteeing minimum service levels for all tenants.
- **Per-tenant observability**: Measuring latency and miss-rate as a function of CKNE tier.

## Code Example
```javascript
function computeAlertParams(ckne, config) {
  return {
    samplingRate: Math.max(1, Math.floor(config.baseSamples * (1 - ckne))),
    queueDepth: config.baseDepth + ckne * config.depthSpan,
    cooldownMs: config.baseCooldown + ckne * config.coolSpan
  };
}

## Why This Is Asked
Edge gateways must enforce multi-tenant QoS using dynamic health signals. This tests a practical, beginner-friendly CKNE rate-limiting pattern that combines health signals with per-tenant control.

## Key Concepts
- CKNE-to-capacity mapping and fairness
- Per-tenant token bucket implementation
- Minute-by-minute degradation loop with cooldowns
- Minimal payloads and testability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal token bucket per tenant
class TB {
  constructor(baseRate){ this.baseRate=baseRate; this.ckne=1; this.tokens=baseRate; this.last=Date.now(); }
  refill(now){
    const dt=(now-this.last)/1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.tokens + dt * this.baseRate * this.ckne, this.baseRate);
    this.last = now;
  }
  tryConsume(n=1){
    this.refill(Date.now());
    if(this.tokens >= n){ this.tokens -= n; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

